course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 16
title: Can he come out? Home time — and the school day
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You knock at Sashko's door and his mum opens it: you ask "mozhna VYity?" — can he come out? — and she says "zaraz", in a moment.
explain: Two hours later a voice rings down the street: "DOdomu!" — home! Every kid answers the same way in every country: "shche p'yat khvylYn!", five more minutes, and it works just as badly.
explain: When you score, a grown-up may shout "molodEts!" — well done! — and your mates say "krutO!", cool.
explain: Sashko also goes to school, shkOla, where the teacher is vchYtel (a man) or vchYtelka (a woman), the lesson is urOk, and the best bit is perERva, break.
explain: After school comes domAshka, homework, and the mark you get is OtsInka — and in Ukraine marks go up to twelve.
fact: "mozhna VYity?" means "can (he) come out?" and is what you ask the friend's mum when you knock for him; you can also say "a Sashko vyiDE?", will Sasha come out?
fact: "DOdomu!" means "home!" and is what parents call to bring the kids in.
fact: "shche p'yat khvylYn!" means "five more minutes!" and is the classic answer to "DOdomu!".
fact: "molodEts!" means "well done!" and adults often say it to children who did something good.
fact: "krutO!" means "cool!" or "awesome!"; the word krutyi means cool, while the verb krutyty means to spin.
fact: "shkOla" means school, and a friend may ask "ty v yakiy shkoli?", which school are you at?
fact: "vchYtel" is a man teacher and "vchYtelka" a woman teacher; to her face, children call a teacher by her first name and her father's name, not by "teacher".
fact: "klAs" means a class, a classroom or a year group, and "my v odnomu klasi" means "we're in the same class"; klAs can also mean "great!".
fact: "urOk" means a lesson, and "perERva" means break or playtime.
fact: "domAshka" is the casual word for homework, short for the longer "domashnye zavdannya".
fact: "OtsInka" means a mark or grade; in Ukrainian schools marks run from 1 to 12, and 12 is the best.
exercise: You knock for Sashko and his mum opens the door. What do you ask? | mozhna VYity? | DOdomu! | shche p'yat khvylYn! | molodEts!
exercise: What does "mozhna VYity?" mean? | Can he come out? | Can I come in? | May I go home? | Is he at school?
exercise: Sashko's mum answers "zaraz". What should you do? | Wait a moment, he is coming | Go home, he can't come out | Come in and take your shoes off | Shout for him louder
exercise: A voice calls "DOdomu!" down the street. What is it saying? | Home! Time to come in | Dinner is ready on the table | Come out and play! | Watch out, a car!
exercise: Sashko's mum calls "DOdomu!". What does Sashko shout back, like every kid? | shche p'yat khvylYn! | DObryi DEN! | krutO! | molodEts!
exercise: What does "shche p'yat khvylYn!" mean? | Five more minutes! | Five more goals! | I'm five years old! | Five o'clock already!
exercise: You do a great trick and Sashko's dad says "molodEts!". What did he say? | Well done! | Be careful! | Go home now! | Who are you?
exercise: What do you answer when someone says "molodEts!" to you? | DYAkuyu! | DOdomu! | ne znAyu | BUvai
exercise: Sashko sees your new bike and says "krutO!". What does he mean? | Cool! Awesome! | It's broken | It spins too much | It's mine now
exercise: What is "shkOla"? | School | A shelf for books | A schoolbag | A school bus
exercise: What is the difference between "vchYtel" and "vchYtelka"? | A man teacher and a woman teacher | A teacher and a head teacher | A teacher and a pupil | A lesson and a teacher
exercise: Sashko says "my v odnomu klasi" about a boy. What does klAs mean here? | A class, a year group | A classic game | A glass of juice | A clash, a fight
exercise: What is "urOk"? | A lesson | A ring for the bell | A break | A test
exercise: The bell goes and everyone runs out shouting "perERva!". What is it? | Break, playtime | The end of the year | A fire drill | Lunch at home
exercise: Sashko's mum asks "ty zrobyv domAshku?". What does she want to know? | Did you do your homework? | Did you go home? | Did you tidy the house? | Did you eat at home?
exercise: What is "OtsInka"? | A mark or grade | A pencil case | A school uniform | A lesson timetable
test: my mate says when Sashko's mum says "zaraz" she means "no, go away". right? | No, zaraz means in a moment, so wait | Yes, zaraz is a polite no | Yes, it means he's grounded | No, it means come inside and eat
test: Ugh, it's freezing, the street lamps are on, and Sashko's mum yells "DOdomu!". What does she want? | Sashko to come home now | Sashko to fetch his coat | You to come in for tea | Everyone to play one more game
test: In a cartoon, a boy answers "DOdomu!" with "shche p'yat khvylYn!" and his mum agrees at once. What usually happens in real life? | It works as badly as in English | Mums always give exactly five minutes | Mums give an hour instead | Ukrainian kids never ask for more time
test: Sashko got a 12 and his cousin says that's bad because "out of 100 it's rubbish". Who's right? | Sashko: 12 is the top mark in Ukraine | The cousin: 12 is a low mark | Both: 12 is an average mark | Neither: Ukraine has no marks
test: Your mate thinks "krutO" means "spinning round" because the verb krutyty means to spin. What do kids mean by "krutO!"? | Cool! Awesome! | Dizzy, about to fall | Round, like a ball | Hot, like soup
test: You want to speak to Sashko's teacher, a woman. What do Ukrainian kids call her to her face? | Her first name and her father's name | Just "vchYtelka" | Her surname with no title | "Mum" by mistake
test: bruv asks why everyone legged it at perERva. what's perERva? | Break, playtime | Home time | A test | The head's office
test: Sashko groans "domAshka..." on Sunday evening. What is he groaning about? | Homework | Housework at home | A bad mark | Going home
test: Someone says "klAs!" after you tell them good news. What can it mean here? | Great! | Classroom! | Be quiet! | A lesson starts!
test: "u nas urOk" — Sashko can't chat now. Why not? | He has a lesson | He has break | He has gone home | He has a bad mark
test: Your little brother scores and Sashko's granny claps: "molodEts!". Is she telling him off? | No, she is praising him | Yes, he kicked too hard | Yes, it means stop it | No, she is asking his name
exam: Determine the most appropriate enquiry to be addressed to a parent at the threshold when one desires that the said parent's child should be permitted to join outdoor play. | mozhna VYity? | DOdomu! | shche p'yat khvylYn! | molodEts!
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the exclamation "DOdomu!" when uttered by a parent in a residential street. | A summons for the children to return home | An invitation for visitors to enter the house | A warning concerning approaching traffic | A declaration that a meal has concluded
exam: It is given that pupils in Ukraine are assessed upon a scale. Select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning the mark of twelve. | It is the highest mark attainable | It is the lowest passing mark | It denotes a failing performance | It is awarded only to teachers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between "vchYtel" and "vchYtelka"? | The former denotes a male teacher, the latter a female teacher | The former denotes a lesson, the latter a teacher | The former denotes a pupil, the latter a teacher | The former denotes a school, the latter a classroom
